# The Aether release aeo is built and tested against.
#
# One bare version string (last non-comment line). Read it the way aeb's
# tooling does:
#
#     grep -v '^#' AETHER_PIN | tr -d '[:space:]'
#
# WHAT THIS IS. aeo is written in Aether: `ae build` compiles bin/aeo.ae,
# bin/aeo-agent.ae and the specs. This names the toolchain that combination
# is known to work with. It is a FLOOR, not a preference — anything newer on
# PATH is accepted as-is, and you should NOT be forced to re-fetch just
# because a nicer release exists.
#
# WHY aeo carries ONE file where aeb carries two. aeb splits AETHER_PIN
# (the floor its own sources need) from AETHER_FETCH (a known-good release
# to download when the floor is unmet) because aeb *fetches a toolchain on
# a user's behalf*. aeo does not: it assumes an `ae` already on PATH and
# fails loudly if it is too old. With nothing to download there is no
# second number to choose, so a single floor is the honest shape. If aeo
# ever grows a bootstrap that installs a toolchain, split this then — and
# copy aeb's rationale wholesale rather than reinventing it.
#
# WHEN TO BUMP. Aether cuts releases fast (seven on 2026-07-26 alone).
# Chasing that is a full-time tic — don't. Move this number on EVIDENCE:
#   - aeo starts calling a primitive an older Aether lacks, or
#   - a release fixes a portability/correctness problem aeo actually hit.
# Bump it in the same commit that introduces the dependency, never
# speculatively.
#
# 0.541.0 — the current floor. Two evidence-backed reasons, not novelty:
#
#   1. std.spec (0.538.0). Aether ABSORBED the standalone `aeocha` BDD
#      framework into the stdlib: std.spec (core + fluent chain),
#      std.os.testing (process matchers) and std.http.client.httptest
#      (HTTP matchers); the aeocha repo is retired. All 78 of aeo's specs
#      import std.spec, so an Aether below 0.538.0 CANNOT COMPILE THE
#      SUITE. This is a hard floor, not a preference.
#   2. std.mutation (0.540.0) + the FreeBSD self-path fix (0.540.0):
#      `ae build`/`ae run` locate the running executable via the
#      KERN_PROC_PATHNAME sysctl instead of Linux-only /proc/self/exe, so
#      they work on a stock FreeBSD install with no linprocfs mounted.
#      aeo's whole FreeBSD line (jails, bhyve, the GhostBSD box) builds
#      there, so this is load-bearing for a first-class substrate.
#
# NOT BUMPED TO 0.542.0 — and that is this file's policy working, not an
# oversight. 0.542.0 landed the optional why-message on the fluent matchers
# (#1576, aeo's own ask: `to_equal_str(want, "why this matters")`). It is
# genuinely wanted, and aeo will adopt it incrementally. But it is BACKWARD
# COMPATIBLE by construction — Aether default args, so aeo's ~810 existing
# message-less call sites compile and pass untouched (verified against the
# PR build before it merged: 48 files / 304 assertions / 0 failures). A
# floor is "the oldest Aether that can build aeo", not "the newest one we
# like". Nothing in the tree REQUIRES 0.542.0 yet, so raising it would
# force a needless upgrade on anyone sitting between the two.
#
# RAISE IT to 0.542.0 in the same commit that first writes a why-message
# into a spec — at that moment the tree really does need it. (An older ae
# would then fail with an arity error on the 3-arg matcher call.)
#
# GLIBC — a SEPARATE concern worth knowing. Aether releases up to ~0.449
# were built on ubuntu-latest and carry a GLIBC_2.38 floor, so `aetherc`
# dies on Debian 12 (glibc 2.36) while `ae --version` still succeeds —
# a version-only probe reports a healthy toolchain that cannot build.
# Aether's release.yml now pins ubuntu-22.04 (glibc 2.35). 0.541.0 is on
# the good side of that line, which matters because aeo's fleet includes
# Debian-class boxes (the Pi 5, the Pi Zero 2 W).
0.541.0
